<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    ===================================================================================
    PREVAST SERVER - CONDITIONS (TIMED BUFFS, DEBUFFS AND AFFLICTIONS)
    ===================================================================================

    VISUALS (per stage). Two different questions - what the player looks like
    WHILE the stage runs, and what it leaves behind when the stage ENDS:

      <visuals skin="N"/>       drawn for the duration of the stage
        skin="1"   the ghoul-drug look (day-skin1)
        skin="2"   the lapadone withdrawal look (day-skin2)
        omitted    no skin change; a stage can modify without showing anything

      <visuals endSkin="N"/>    left behind when the stage's duration expires
        endSkin="0"   back to the plain skin
        endSkin="4"   the used-lapadone look, until something cures it
        omitted       leave whatever mark is already there

      <visuals poisonScreenEffect="true"/>  green screen distortion, wearer only

    Those are the only values, and unrecognised ones are reported at startup -
    including the ones that look like they should work. skin="0" is not a thing
    to write (omit the skin instead), and endSkin="1" / endSkin="2" cannot exist
    because both of those need a LIVE timer to be drawn at all.

    endSkin is why the withdrawal stage below keeps its pink skin after it ends.
    Delete the attribute and lapadone wears off to the normal skin instead.

    REPELLENTS (per stage):
    - <repel families="ghoul"/> makes the wearer invisible to every agent whose
      agents.xml family= matches, for as long as that stage is active. They will
      not select, alert about, swing at or retaliate against them at all; agents
      of any OTHER family are completely unaffected.
    - <repel keys="halbot"/> does the same for one named agent type.
    - Both attributes take comma-separated lists and may be combined; a name
      that matches nothing in agents.xml is reported at startup.

    MODIFIERS (per stage, inside <modifiers>):
    - <speed multiplier= add=/>          movement speed
    - <healthRegen multiplier=/>         scales the life gauge's speedInc
    - <staminaRegen multiplier=/>        scales the stamina gauge's speedInc
    - <staminaDrain multiplier=/>        scales the stamina gauge's speedDec
    The three multipliers ride out on the gauge RATES the client integrates, so
    both simulations use the same numbers. There is no maxHealth modifier: life
    is a 0-255 gauge whose ceiling modes.xml already sets to 255, so a bonus has
    nowhere to go. Writing one is reported at startup rather than ignored.

    GAUGE TICKS - damage, healing or drain over time, one per gauge:
    - <healthTick    amount= intervalMs=/>   negative hurts, positive heals
    - <foodTick      amount= intervalMs=/>   a parasite, or a slow feed
    - <warmthTick    amount= intervalMs=/>   chill, or a lingering warmth
    - <staminaTick   amount= intervalMs=/>   exhaustion, or a stimulant
    - <radiationTick amount= intervalMs=/>   contamination, or a decon drip
    intervalMs below 50 is reported at startup: the effect loop runs at 50 ms and
    cannot fire faster, so a smaller number is silently the same as 50. An AGENT
    has only a health pool, so the other four simply do nothing to one - which is
    what lets the same condition be written once and applied to anybody.

    COMBAT (per stage, inside <modifiers>):
    - <damageDealt multiplier=/>    scales damage this creature INFLICTS (0.1..3)
    - <damageTaken multiplier=/>    scales damage this creature RECEIVES (0.1..3)
    - <critChance add=/>            added to the wielder's crit chance
    - <knockbackResist fraction=/>  0..1 of incoming knockback shrugged off
    - <leech life= stamina=/>       a vampirism BUFF, on top of the weapon's own
    Both damage multipliers are clamped to 0.1..3.0 with a warning: they are the
    easiest knobs here to make a degenerate item with.

    CONTROL (per stage, inside <modifiers>) - what the stage FORBIDS:
    - <control move= turn= run= attack= use= build= /> (all default "true")
    Write "false" to take something away. A <control> that forbids nothing is
    reported at startup. move="false" implies run="false". This is how a stun or
    a paralyse is written - NOT as <speed multiplier="0"/>, which cannot work:
    speed is floored well above zero, so the most a multiplier can do is a crawl,
    and it cannot stop an attack at all.

    EVERY control bit forbids something the creature does OF ITS OWN ACCORD, and
    nothing the world does TO it. A stunned player still takes damage, still
    catches conditions, and is STILL KNOCKED BACK - being shoved is done to you,
    so a stunned player can be knocked out of a doorway rather than becoming a
    statue. `turn` is separate from `move` for the same reason: a paralysed
    player is rooted but still a threat and can turn to shoot, where a stunned
    one has lost the plot entirely.

    RESISTANCE TO OTHER CONDITIONS (per stage, inside <modifiers>):
    - <resist conditions="a,b" fraction="0.5" all="0.2"/>
    A fraction SHRINKS THE DURATION of the named conditions, and 1.0 (the
    default for fraction=) blocks them outright. Duration rather than a chance to
    block, so a protection reads the same way every time it applies instead of
    being a lottery. `all=` is the blanket form. Sources compose as a product of
    what each lets through, so two halvings leave a quarter and never reach
    immunity by accident. A player's WEARABLE answers the same question through
    <modifier key="<conditionKey>Resistance">, and an agent's through
    <resistances><condition key= fraction=/></resistances> in agents.xml.

    STACKING (<stacking mode="..."/>), what a second dose does while the first
    is still running:
    - "refresh" (the default) restarts the effect from its first stage
    - "extend"  keeps the current stage and adds to the time left
    - "ignore"  does nothing
    Anything else is reported at startup; it used to mean "ignore" in silence.

    NAME: <condition name="..."> is the human label. Nothing on the wire carries
    it - the client has no notion of a named condition - so it appears only in
    the startup warnings and in the admin effects inspector.

    TAGS: <condition tags="poison,disease"> is what the condition IS, so a cure
    can name a KIND rather than enumerate keys - <cureCondition tags="poison"/>
    keeps working when a sixth poison is added, where keys="a,b,c,d,e" does not.
    That list has rotted once already: lapadoine_effect was renamed and the
    antidote silently stopped curing it. A cure name may be a key or a tag.

    WHAT APPLIES ONE:
    - an item eaten:   <condition key="..."/> inside <consumable> (equipables.xml)
    - a weapon hit:    <onHit condition= chance= chancePerDamage=/> inside <damage>
    - a round landing: <onHit .../> on a <projectile> (projectiles.xml)
    - an agent's bite: <ability condition= chance=/> (agents.xml)
    - a critical hit:  <crit condition= conditionChance=/> on any of those
    chancePerDamage adds to the chance per point of damage that ACTUALLY landed,
    so a big hit is likelier to stun than a graze - and armour, which reduced the
    damage, reduces the chance with it.

    EVERY source can override HOW LONG and HOW MUCH, so one condition serves any
    number of sources instead of needing a near-duplicate per weapon:
      durationMs="3000"   this source's own length, replacing the FIRST stage's.
                          Later stages keep their own - those are the effect's
                          shape, not the dose's.
      strength="1.4"      how hard, as a multiple of what is written below.
                          1.0 is the condition exactly as stated; 0.4 is a
                          fraction of it; 1.8 is nearly twice.
    (On a <crit> the two are spelled conditionDurationMs= and conditionStrength=,
    so the crit's own chance and duration cannot be confused with the hit's.)

    strength scales every MAGNITUDE and no SWITCH. Speeds, gauge ticks, damage
    multipliers, leech, crit chance and resistances all move with it; <control>,
    the drug skins and <repel> do not - half a stun is not a thing. A multiplier
    scales by its DISTANCE FROM 1, so <speed multiplier="0.55"/> at strength 2 is
    x0.10 (twice as slow), not x1.10. strength="0" and negatives are refused at
    startup: they would apply an inert condition, or invert it into a boost.

    An item removes them with <cureCondition keys="a,b"/> (keys or tags, or the
    "all" sentinel).
    ===================================================================================
-->
<conditions>
    <!-- Ghoul Drug: Grants Ghoul Repellent (day-skin1.png).
         Ghouls ignore the wearer entirely for the duration; robots (family
         "bot") are not fooled and keep hunting normally. -->
    <condition key="ghoul_drug_effect" name="Ghoul Repellent Drug" removeOnDeath="true">
        <stacking mode="refresh"/>
        <stage key="active" durationMs="600000">
            <visuals skin="1"/>
            <repel families="ghoul"/>
        </stage>
    </condition>

    <!-- Lapadone: 6s Speed Boost (no skin change) -> 6s Withdrawal (day-skin2.png)
         -> Normal Skin (day-skin0.png).

         There is no "boost" skin to ask for. The protocol carries exactly two
         drug visuals - opcode 68 (onRepellent, the ghoul drug) and opcode 69
         (onLapadoine, which sets the client field named `withdrawal`) - so
         skin="1" and skin="2" are the only values statuseffect.cpp accepts.
         The boost previously sent skin="2", i.e. the withdrawal look, during
         the buff; the withdrawal stage sent skin="4", which matches no branch
         and fell through to no skin at all. -->
    <condition key="lapadone_effect" name="Lapadone" removeOnDeath="true">
        <stacking mode="refresh"/>

        <!-- Stage 1: Lapadone Speed Boost (+35% speed).

             No endSkin here on purpose: this stage hands off to `withdrawal`
             rather than ending the condition, and what the whole course leaves
             behind is the withdrawal stage's business. endSkin does fire on a
             handing-off stage if you ask for one; this one just does not want
             it. -->
        <stage key="boost" durationMs="6000" nextStage="withdrawal">
            <modifiers>
                <speed multiplier="1.35"/>
                <staminaRegen multiplier="1.25"/>
            </modifiers>
            <visuals skin="2"/>
        </stage>

        <!-- Stage 2: Lapadone Withdrawal Penalty (-30% speed, Day Skin 2).

             endSkin="4" is what makes the pink skin OUTLIVE the withdrawal: the
             speed penalty stops after 6s but the wearer keeps the look until an
             antidote clears it, which is what the antidote advertises. Delete
             the attribute and lapadone wears off to the normal skin instead. -->
        <stage key="withdrawal" durationMs="6000">
            <modifiers>
                <speed multiplier="0.70"/>
                <staminaRegen multiplier="0.65"/>
            </modifiers>
            <visuals skin="2" endSkin="4"/>
        </stage>
    </condition>

    <!-- Toxic Poison (Rotten Mushroom): Deals damage over 1 minute -->
    <condition key="toxic_poison" name="Toxic Poison" tags="poison" removeOnDeath="true">
        <stacking mode="refresh"/>
        <stage key="active" durationMs="60000">
            <modifiers>
                <speed multiplier="0.85"/>
                <healthTick amount="-5" intervalMs="2000"/>
            </modifiers>
            <visuals poisonScreenEffect="true"/>
        </stage>
    </condition>

    <!--
        ===============================================================================
        BELOW: conditions added with the combat-effects work. Every number here is a
        FIRST GUESS and exists to make the mechanism reachable from content - tune
        them in play. Nothing in the shipped world applies these except where noted.
        ===============================================================================
    -->

    <!-- Stun: cannot move or attack at all, briefly.

         Written as <control>, NOT as <speed multiplier="0"/>. Speed is floored
         well above zero, so a multiplier can only ever produce a crawl, and it
         cannot stop an attack under any value. Keep the duration SHORT: this is
         the strongest thing in the file, and a long one is not a fight. -->
    <condition key="stun" name="Stunned" tags="control" removeOnDeath="true">
        <stacking mode="refresh"/>
        <stage key="active" durationMs="1200">
            <modifiers>
                <control move="false" turn="false" attack="false" use="false" build="false"/>
            </modifiers>
        </stage>
    </condition>

    <!-- Paralysis: rooted, but can still fight back. The slower, fairer sibling
         of a stun - it takes position away rather than agency. -->
    <condition key="paralysis" name="Paralysed" tags="control" removeOnDeath="true">
        <stacking mode="refresh"/>
        <stage key="active" durationMs="2500">
            <modifiers>
                <control move="false"/>
            </modifiers>
        </stage>
    </condition>

    <!-- Crippled: a leg wound. Walks, cannot run, and heals slowly. -->
    <condition key="crippled" name="Crippled" tags="wound" removeOnDeath="true">
        <stacking mode="refresh"/>
        <stage key="active" durationMs="15000">
            <modifiers>
                <speed multiplier="0.6"/>
                <control run="false"/>
                <healthRegen multiplier="0.5"/>
            </modifiers>
        </stage>
    </condition>

    <!-- Slowed: the plain "you got caught in something" movement penalty. A
         blast wave, a gas cloud, a net. No control bits - you can still run,
         you are just worse at it - which is what makes it the right thing for
         an explosion to leave behind rather than a stun.

         Resisted by <modifier key="slowedResistance"> on a wearable: the key is
         the condition key plus "Resistance", and nothing in the code knows
         either name. -->
    <condition key="slowed" name="Slowed" tags="control,movement" removeOnDeath="true">
        <stacking mode="refresh"/>
        <stage key="active" durationMs="4000">
            <modifiers>
                <speed multiplier="0.55"/>
            </modifiers>
        </stage>
    </condition>

    <!-- Bleeding: damage over time that a bandage should cure. Tagged `wound`
         so <cureCondition tags="wound"/> covers this and anything like it. -->
    <condition key="bleeding" name="Bleeding" tags="wound,bleed" removeOnDeath="true">
        <stacking mode="refresh"/>
        <stage key="active" durationMs="10000">
            <modifiers>
                <healthTick amount="-3" intervalMs="1000"/>
            </modifiers>
        </stage>
    </condition>

    <!-- Adrenaline: fast and hard-hitting, then the crash. What a stimulant
         should feel like - the second stage is the price of the first. -->
    <condition key="adrenaline" name="Adrenaline" tags="drug" removeOnDeath="true">
        <stacking mode="refresh"/>
        <stage key="rush" durationMs="8000" nextStage="crash">
            <modifiers>
                <speed multiplier="1.25"/>
                <damageDealt multiplier="1.3"/>
                <critChance add="0.15"/>
                <staminaDrain multiplier="0.5"/>
                <knockbackResist fraction="0.5"/>
            </modifiers>
        </stage>
        <stage key="crash" durationMs="12000">
            <modifiers>
                <speed multiplier="0.8"/>
                <damageDealt multiplier="0.8"/>
                <staminaTick amount="-2" intervalMs="1000"/>
            </modifiers>
        </stage>
    </condition>

    <!-- Vampirism: leech with whatever you are holding, stacking on top of a
         weapon that already leeches. -->
    <condition key="vampirism" name="Vampirism" tags="drug" removeOnDeath="true">
        <stacking mode="refresh"/>
        <stage key="active" durationMs="20000">
            <modifiers>
                <leech life="0.2"/>
            </modifiers>
        </stage>
    </condition>

    <!-- Fortified: takes less, moves slower. The defensive counterpart. -->
    <condition key="fortified" name="Fortified" tags="buff" removeOnDeath="true">
        <stacking mode="refresh"/>
        <stage key="active" durationMs="15000">
            <modifiers>
                <damageTaken multiplier="0.6"/>
                <speed multiplier="0.85"/>
                <knockbackResist fraction="0.8"/>
            </modifiers>
        </stage>
    </condition>

    <!-- Antitoxin: protection FROM conditions rather than a condition's own
         effect. Blocks poison outright and shortens everything else, which is
         what a medical counter-agent should read as. -->
    <condition key="antitoxin" name="Antitoxin" tags="buff" removeOnDeath="true">
        <stacking mode="refresh"/>
        <stage key="active" durationMs="45000">
            <modifiers>
                <resist conditions="toxic_poison,bleeding" fraction="1.0" all="0.3"/>
            </modifiers>
        </stage>
    </condition>

    <!-- Irradiated: fills the radiation gauge from a source that is not a field,
         so a weapon or a bite can contaminate you where only geography could
         before. -->
    <condition key="irradiated" name="Irradiated" tags="radiation" removeOnDeath="true">
        <stacking mode="extend"/>
        <stage key="active" durationMs="20000">
            <modifiers>
                <radiationTick amount="4" intervalMs="1000"/>
            </modifiers>
        </stage>
    </condition>

</conditions>
